+## Stock allocation
+
+<a id="st-1"></a>
+### ST-1. Checkout completion ignores every existing allocation, so stock is oversold
+
+- **What happens:** `allocate_stocks` builds the list of stock ids as a one-shot generator. When
+  reservations are checked, the reservation query consumes it. The allocation query that follows
+  gets an empty list and returns nothing, so availability is computed as if nothing were allocated.
+  Two checkouts for the last unit both succeed. Allocations also pile onto the first-sorted
+  warehouse beyond its physical stock, and staff fulfilling from it get "insufficient stock".
+- **When:** every time reservations are checked. Storefront checkout always passes
+  `check_reservations=True`. Draft completion does so when stock reservation is enabled in site
+  settings. A back office that only uses draft orders with reservations off is not affected by this
+  path, but shares stock with any storefront that is.
+- **Where:** `saleor/warehouse/management.py:150-163` and `243-265`; callers
+  `saleor/checkout/complete_checkout.py:840-851`, `1296-1307`;
+  `saleor/graphql/order/mutations/draft_order_complete.py:236-246`.
+- **Status:** Reproduced. With the reservation filter first the allocation query returned `[]`;
+  without it, the real sum.
+
+### ST-2. Several lines of the same product in one checkout each see the full stock
+
+- **What happens:** the allocation loop reads how much is already allocated but never adds what it
+  has just allocated for the previous line. Three lines of 2 against a stock of 5 all succeed: 6
+  allocated from 5.
+- **When:** every time a checkout holds the same variant on more than one line.
+- **Where:** `saleor/warehouse/management.py:185-195`, `316-356`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### ST-3. Changing a line quantity twice at once leaves stock allocated forever
+
+- **What happens:** `orderLineUpdate` reads the old quantity before taking the order lock and does
+  not re-read it. Two overlapping updates from 5 to 8 each add 3. The line says 8, the allocation
+  says 11. Fulfillment releases only what is fulfilled, so 3 units stay allocated; the cleanup task
+  only deletes zero rows.
+- **When:** concurrent, on unconfirmed orders.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/order/mutations/order_line_update.py:49`, `93-108`;
+  `saleor/order/utils.py:529-556`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### ST-4. Adding a line with too little stock returns "Internal Server Error"
+
+- **What happens:** on an unconfirmed order, `orderLinesCreate` allocates stock immediately. The
+  resulting `InsufficientStock` is not caught; only tax errors are. The employee gets a top-level
+  internal error with no field message. `orderLineUpdate` handles the same case properly.
+- **When:** every time.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/order/mutations/order_lines_create.py:160-177`;
+  `saleor/order/utils.py:329-344`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+<a id="st-5"></a>
+### ST-5. Out-of-stock and back-in-stock webhooks fire for the wrong stock
+
+- **What happens:** the per-stock webhooks are registered in a loop as
+  `transaction.on_commit(lambda: trigger_...(stock, ...))`. The lambda reads the loop variable when
+  the transaction commits, so every callback sees the last item. The stock that actually ran out
+  gets no event; the last one gets several. Separately, fulfilling from an already-empty stock
+  re-sends "out of stock" each time, because there is no before/after comparison.
+- **When:** every time an allocation, fulfillment, cancel, expiry, refund or return touches more
+  than one stock and the one that changed state is not the last.
+- **Where:** `saleor/warehouse/management.py:219-231`, `426-438`, `644-648`, `789-795`.
+- **Status:** Reproduced. Three lambdas registered in a loop all fired with the last value.
+
+### ST-6. The allocated total on a stock drifts when one fulfillment releases two allocations of it
+
+- **What happens:** each allocation row carries its own copy of the stock. Each copy gets
+  "allocated minus n" and both go into one bulk update, which applies only the first. Deallocating
+  6 and 4 from 10 leaves 4.
+- **When:** every time an order has two lines of the same variant allocated in one warehouse and
+  both are fulfilled together.
+- **Effect:** `Stock.quantityAllocated` shown to staff is too high until the nightly reconciliation,
+  and stock-update mutations that read this field misreport in/out of stock meanwhile.
+- **Where:** `saleor/warehouse/management.py:402-406`, `449`.
+- **Status:** Reproduced.
+
+### ST-7. Deleting a stock entry or a warehouse silently drops the reservations of open orders
+
+*Corrected on re-examination. The first version ended "the quantity can be sold again". That was
+wrong as written: the removed entry's quantity is deleted with it, so nothing can be sold from it.*
+
+- **What happens:** the stock-entry delete, the warehouse delete and the stock removal in
+  `productVariantBulkUpdate` do not check for allocations. `Stock.warehouse`, `Allocation.stock` and
+  `Reservation.stock` all cascade. Deleting the entry removes its quantity and, with it, every
+  allocation and checkout reservation held against it. Open orders keep their lines and still show
+  the items as to be fulfilled, but nothing is reserved for them any more. No error is returned and
+  no event is written on the affected orders.
+- **Can the removed stock be resold?** Not from the removed entry. Availability is the sum of the
+  stock rows that exist, minus their allocations; a deleted row contributes nothing. With no other
+  stock for the product, new orders and checkouts are refused as out of stock.
+- **Where overselling does come from:**
+  - *Other warehouses.* If the product has stock elsewhere, the affected orders no longer subtract
+    anything from it. All of it shows as available and can be sold to new customers, while the
+    affected orders still need units. Total open demand can then exceed what exists.
+  - *Re-entering the goods.* When the same physical units are entered again, as a new stock entry
+    in the same warehouse or in another one after a move, the new row starts with nothing
+    allocated. The full count is available, including the units that were promised to open orders.
+    This is the case where already-promised goods are sold a second time, and it is the natural
+    next step after closing a warehouse or deleting an entry to correct it.
+  - Nothing re-creates the lost allocation. Raising the line quantity on an unconfirmed order
+    allocates only the increase.
+- **What staff see afterwards:**
+  - Fulfilling an affected order from the deleted warehouse fails with "insufficient stock". From
+    another warehouse it succeeds only if that warehouse has unreserved stock.
+  - With `allowStockToBeExceeded` it goes through without decreasing any stock.
+  - A fulfillment that was waiting for approval loses its stock link (`FulfillmentLine.stock` is set
+    to null) and can no longer be approved, except with `allowStockToBeExceeded`, which again
+    decreases nothing.
+  - Past fulfillments lose the record of which stock entry they shipped from.
+- **When:** every time an entry or warehouse with allocated stock is deleted.
+- **Where:** `saleor/warehouse/models.py:528`, `581-587`, `676-682` (cascades), `330-345`
+  (availability); `saleor/warehouse/management.py:60-67`, `542-545`, `669-681`;
+  `saleor/graphql/product/bulk_mutations/product_variant_stocks_delete.py:108-134`;
+  `saleor/graphql/warehouse/mutations/warehouse_delete.py:72-98`;
+  `saleor/graphql/product/bulk_mutations/product_variant_bulk_update.py:745-746`;
+  `saleor/order/models.py:866-872`; `saleor/order/actions.py:887-898`, `1164-1179`.
+- **Status:** Verified by reading, including the absence of any allocation check in the three
+  delete paths. Not run. Deleting without a guard may be intended; the silent loss of reservations
+  is the problem.
+
+### ST-8. Smaller items
+
+- Editing stock through `productVariantBulkUpdate` or `stockBulkUpdate` sends no out-of-stock or
+  back-in-stock events; the same edit through `productVariantStocksUpdate` does. Traced,
+  `saleor/graphql/product/bulk_mutations/product_variant_bulk_update.py:726-790`,
+  `saleor/graphql/warehouse/bulk_mutations/stock_bulk_update.py:384-409`.
+- Stock locks are taken in different orders by draft completion, line creation, restock and
+  checkout. Two of them on the same products can deadlock. Plausible.
+- `delete_empty_allocations_task` lists zero-quantity allocations, then deletes by id without
+  re-checking the quantity. A restock in between reuses the row and it is deleted while holding
+  stock. `update_stocks_quantity_allocated_task` computes its correction before writing and
+  overwrites concurrent changes. Verified for the missing re-check, `saleor/warehouse/tasks.py:16-63`,
+  `saleor/warehouse/management.py:80-87`. The timing is Plausible.
+- Click-and-collect points set to "all warehouses" are offered based on stock in warehouses outside
+  the order's channel; completion then fails. Traced, `saleor/warehouse/models.py:198-246`.
+- Legacy preorder code has further defects (`saleor/warehouse/management.py:993-1015`,
+  `saleor/warehouse/reservations.py:323`). They matter only if preorder data already exists.
