+## Transactions API
+
+<a id="tx-1"></a>
+### TX-1. A payment report that fails half-way is never applied; the retry is told "already processed"
+
+- **What happens:** `transactionEventReport` saves the event in its own transaction. Recalculating
+  the transaction amounts and updating the order happen afterwards, in separate steps. If the pod
+  dies or an error is raised in between, the event exists but the order was never updated. When the
+  app retries, the duplicate check finds the event and returns `alreadyProcessed: true` without
+  recalculating anything. The employee sees a successful charge event on an unpaid order. It heals
+  only if another event later arrives for that transaction.
+- **When:** whenever the first attempt is interrupted after the event is saved: a pod restart, a
+  database error, or the gift-card error in FU-9.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/transaction/transaction_event_report.py:398-478`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### TX-2. "Fully paid" side effects can fire twice
+
+- **What happens:** whether to run the paid handling is decided from the change in the transaction's
+  charged amount, using a value read at the start of the request. Nothing asks whether the order was
+  already fully paid. When a charge report overlaps checkout completion, or two reports for one
+  transaction are processed together, `ORDER_PAID`, `ORDER_FULLY_PAID` and `ORDER_UPDATED` are sent
+  twice, two "fully paid" history events are written, the payment confirmation email goes out
+  twice, and gift-card auto-fulfillment is attempted twice (which raises, see FU-9, and then leads
+  to TX-1). Refund events duplicate the same way.
+- **When:** concurrent.
+- **Where:** `saleor/order/actions.py:697-706`, `716-783`; `saleor/payment/utils.py:1334-1427`;
+  `transaction_event_report.py:440-462`.
+- **Status:** Verified for the delta-based decision. The interleaving is Traced.
+
+### TX-3. Staff-requested actions are silently altered and not serialized
+
+- **What happens:** `transactionRequestAction` caps the amount at the current charged or authorized
+  value, read without a lock, and reports success either way. A refund of 50 on a transaction with
+  30 charged becomes 30 with no error. A refund on a transaction with nothing charged sends a
+  zero-amount request and reports success. A request in flight is not counted until the payment app
+  answers, so a second click sends a second request (RR-2). A transaction whose checkout was deleted
+  and has no order raises an internal error.
+- **When:** every time for the clamping; concurrent for the duplicate.
+- **Where:** `transaction_request_action.py:105-156`, `268-274`.
+- **Status:** Verified for the clamping and the missing lock.
+
+### TX-4. `transactionUpdate` can reset a paid transaction and double-count amounts
+
+- **What happens:** the mutation saves the whole transaction row from a copy read at the start, and
+  recalculates amounts only when the call includes money fields. An update that changes only
+  metadata, the message or available actions, while a charge report is being processed elsewhere,
+  writes the old amounts back: charged returns to 0 and the order shows unpaid until another event
+  arrives. Separately, "set charged to X" is stored as "add X minus current", computed without a
+  lock, so two identical calls record the charge twice.
+- **When:** concurrent.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/transaction/transaction_update.py:164-175`, `207`;
+  `saleor/payment/utils.py:1698-1811`.
+- **Status:** Verified for the full-row save. The double count is Traced.
+
+### TX-5. Events reported with an empty `pspReference` use different arithmetic
+
+- **What happens:** an empty string is accepted. Such events are handled as manual adjustments: a
+  charge does not reduce the authorized amount, a refund does not reduce the charged amount,
+  requests and failures are ignored, and a refund reversal raises charged without lowering refunded.
+  All events with an empty reference also collide in the duplicate check, so a second charge of the
+  same amount is reported as already processed and never recorded.
+- **When:** every time an app records cash or manual payments with an empty reference.
+- **Effect:** after authorising 100 and charging 100, both read 100. After refunding 100 the order
+  is still fully charged and refundable again.
+- **Where:** `saleor/payment/transaction_item_calculations.py:235-288`;
+  `transaction_event_report.py:93-95`; `saleor/payment/utils.py:1131-1151`.
+- **Status:** Verified for the arithmetic. The acceptance of an empty string is Traced.
+
+### TX-6. Channel restrictions are not enforced when recording payments
+
+- **What happens:** only `transactionRequestAction` and the granted-refund request check channel
+  access. `transactionCreate`, `transactionUpdate` and `transactionEventReport` do not. A staff user
+  limited to one channel, with payment permission, can record a charge on another channel's order
+  and trigger all the fully-paid side effects. The list query for transactions is scoped by channel
+  and owning app; the single `transaction` query is not, so an app can read another app's
+  transaction.
+- **When:** every time.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/transaction/transaction_create.py:340-346`;
+  `transaction_update.py:206-216`; `transaction_event_report.py:318-332`;
+  `saleor/graphql/payment/utils.py:22-46`; `saleor/graphql/payment/resolvers.py:35-73`.
+- **Status:** Verified for the mutations. The query is Traced.
+
+<a id="tx-7"></a>
+### TX-7. Transactions created with `transactionCreate` can be read without logging in
+
+- **What happens:** `transactionProcess` requires no permission; it relies on the transaction's
+  random token being unguessable (ADR 0010). But every transaction made by `transactionCreate` is
+  flagged to also accept its sequential integer id. If the transaction already has a final
+  authorization or charge event, the mutation returns the full transaction before any other check:
+  amounts, provider reference, external URL, all events and the linked order or checkout.
+- **When:** every time, for transactions recorded through `transactionCreate` with an authorized or
+  charged amount. Back-office payments recorded by staff or apps are exactly this kind.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/transaction/transaction_create.py:289-291`;
+  `transaction/utils.py:33-43`; `transaction_process.py:76-82`, `139-146`, `180-188`.
+- **Status:** Verified. ADR 0010 documents the token as a capability; it does not cover the
+  integer-id path.
+
+### TX-8. `transactionInitialize` without an amount asks for too much
+
+- **What happens:** the default amount is the stored checkout or order total minus existing
+  transactions. For a checkout that total is before gift cards, so a 100 checkout with a 30 gift
+  card requests 100, and the gift card is still drawn down at completion. For each transaction only
+  the larger of authorized and charged is subtracted, not their sum, so a partly captured
+  authorization is under-counted.
+- **When:** every time the amount is omitted in those two situations.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/payment/mutations/base.py:104-122`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### TX-9. Payment-app responses are loosely validated
+
+- A response that is valid JSON but not an object (`[]`, `"ok"`) crashes the handler. The requested
+  charge, refund or cancel is left with no outcome and a granted refund stays pending.
+- Negative amounts are accepted from app responses, although GraphQL inputs reject them. A charge of
+  -50 lowers charged and raises authorized.
+- An explicit amount of 0 in a response becomes the full requested amount.
+- Amounts are kept to three decimals whatever the currency, so a checkout can sit at "partially
+  paid" or "overcharged" by a fraction of a cent.
+- **Where:** `saleor/payment/utils.py:853-921`, `993-1022`;
+  `saleor/webhook/response_schemas/transaction.py:152-207`.
+- **Status:** Traced.
+
+### TX-10. Smaller items
+
+- Transaction amounts are recalculated and saved after the row lock is released, from the copy read
+  before it. Two different events for one transaction on two pods can leave a committed event out of
+  the totals until the next event. The same save always rewrites metadata, overwriting concurrent
+  metadata changes. Verified for the lock scope, `transaction_event_report.py:398-452`.
+- `transactionUpdate(amountAuthorized: X)` does not reach X once a charge or cancel with a provider
+  reference exists: the adjustment is applied and the charge is subtracted again. Traced,
+  `saleor/payment/utils.py:1761-1780`.
+- A chargeback or refund reversal reported without an amount takes the amount of the newest event
+  with that provider reference on any transaction, not just its own. Verified,
+  `saleor/payment/utils.py:2071-2077`.
+- A failure response without a provider reference is matched against older failures of the same
+  type. The retry shows no outcome, or a misleading "already exists with different amount". Traced,
+  `saleor/payment/utils.py:1140-1175`.
+- A charge, refund or cancel request can end with no outcome event at all: if the app or webhook was
+  deactivated before the task ran, or the worker died mid-task. Nothing expires such requests.
+  Traced, `saleor/webhook/transport/synchronous/transport.py:74-98`. See RR-6.
+- `orderMarkAsPaid` checks "no transactions yet" without a lock. A double click creates two
+  full-amount transactions and an overcharged order. Verified,
+  `saleor/graphql/order/mutations/order_mark_as_paid.py:81-109`, `saleor/order/actions.py:1054-1063`.
+- The public `Order.transactionSummaries` field hides card digits but not the last characters of a
+  gift-card code. Traced, `saleor/graphql/payment/types.py:641-653`.
