+
+## Webhooks
+
+<a id="wh-1"></a>
+### WH-1. Retries of charge, refund and cancel requests send an empty body
+
+- **What happens:** these deliveries store their payload in a file, with the database column empty.
+  After any failed send, the failure handler calls `save_as_file()`, which reads the (empty) column
+  and writes it to a new file, replacing the real payload. The retry then posts an empty body,
+  correctly signed. A single 5xx from the payment app therefore cannot recover: every retry is
+  empty and the action ends as failed. The delivery shown in the dashboard is empty too, and a
+  manual retry also sends nothing.
+- **When:** every time the payment app answers a `TRANSACTION_CHARGE_REQUESTED`,
+  `TRANSACTION_REFUND_REQUESTED` or `TRANSACTION_CANCELATION_REQUESTED` with a 5xx.
+- **Where:** `saleor/core/models.py:189-209`; `saleor/webhook/transport/utils.py:676-688`;
+  `saleor/webhook/transport/synchronous/transport.py:67-98`, `185-189`, `314-323`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+<a id="wh-2"></a>
+### WH-2. Webhooks installed from an app manifest ignore their channel filter
+
+- **What happens:** a subscription such as `orderUpdated(channels: ["b2b"])` is filtered before
+  dispatch using a column on the webhook, `filterable_channel_slugs`. Only the `webhookCreate` and
+  `webhookUpdate` mutations fill it in. Manifest installation creates the webhook without it, and
+  an empty list means "all channels". The app receives order, draft-order, checkout and stock
+  events for every channel, with full payloads.
+- **When:** every time, for any app installed from a manifest.
+- **Where:** `saleor/app/installation_utils.py:310-322`; `saleor/webhook/utils.py:202-222`; contrast
+  `saleor/graphql/webhook/mutations/webhook_create.py:155-166`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### WH-3. One legacy webhook can lose a price-change event for every app
+
+- **What happens:** `PRODUCT_VARIANT_DISCOUNTED_PRICE_UPDATED` is dispatched with no legacy payload.
+  If any active webhook without a subscription query is subscribed to it, or to `ANY_EVENTS`, the
+  dispatch raises `NotImplementedError`. Prices are already saved, so the next run sees no change
+  and the events are gone for all apps. The recalculation task also dies before clearing its dirty
+  flags.
+- **When:** every time, once such a webhook exists and a promotion or price change is processed.
+- **Where:** `saleor/plugins/webhook/plugin.py:2046-2063`;
+  `saleor/webhook/transport/asynchronous/transport.py:397-405`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### WH-4. `ANY_EVENTS` webhooks never receive order, draft-order or checkout events
+
+- **What happens:** order and checkout code looks webhooks up per event name from a precomputed map.
+  An `ANY_EVENTS` webhook is filed only under `any_events`, which nothing reads. The integration
+  gets product and customer events but no `ORDER_*`, `DRAFT_ORDER_*` or `CHECKOUT_*`, with no error
+  and no delivery row.
+- **When:** every time.
+- **Where:** `saleor/webhook/utils.py:129-199`; `saleor/order/actions.py:257-284`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+<a id="wh-5"></a>
+### WH-5. Deliveries can stay pending forever
+
+- **What happens:** several paths leave a delivery `PENDING` and exit: a worker killed mid-send
+  (BG-3); an exception other than `ValueError` in the send task; the deferred-payload task giving up
+  on a lagging replica; a subscription whose query produces no data for an event it is subscribed
+  to. No scheduled task revisits pending deliveries. Only a manual retry moves one, and that cannot
+  help when the payload was never generated.
+- **When:** on worker restarts, storage errors, or misconfigured subscriptions.
+- **Where:** `saleor/webhook/transport/asynchronous/transport.py:614-632`, `731-750`, `804-857`;
+  `saleor/settings.py:673-769` (no sweeper in the beat schedule).
+- **Status:** Verified for the send task and the schedule. The deferred-payload paths are Traced.
+
+### WH-6. Retrying a failed delivery from the dashboard can repeat a payment action
+
+- **What happens:** `eventDeliveryRetry` accepts any delivery, including failed synchronous ones such
+  as a refund request. It re-sends it through the asynchronous sender and discards the answer. The
+  payment app is asked to refund again and Saleor records nothing. After a 5xx the body is empty
+  (WH-1). There is no check on the delivery's current status, so retrying while the original is
+  still retrying duplicates the send.
+- **When:** every time staff with app-management permission retry such a delivery.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/webhook/mutations/event_delivery_retry.py:31-48`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### WH-7. Any staff member with an event's permission can send that event to any app
+
+- **What happens:** `webhookTrigger` requires only an authenticated staff user plus the permission of
+  the event itself, not app management. It creates a real, signed delivery to the production target
+  for an arbitrary existing object. Staff with order permission can replay `ORDER_CREATED` for an old
+  order into the ERP.
+- **When:** every time.
+- **Where:** `saleor/graphql/webhook/mutations/webhook_trigger.py:56-58`, `116-148`.
+- **Status:** Verified.
+
+### WH-8. A tax-app response with no `lines` key is accepted and zeroes the order subtotal
+
+- **What happens:** the response schema defaults `lines` to an empty list, and the line-count check
+  is a field validator, which does not run on defaults. The result is applied with a non-strict
+  `zip`, so no line is updated, the subtotal is set to 0 and the total to shipping only. No tax
+  error is recorded. An explicit empty list is rejected correctly.
+- **When:** every time the tax app returns 200 with shipping fields only.
+- **Where:** `saleor/webhook/response_schemas/taxes.py:15-35`; `saleor/order/calculations.py:575-634`.
+- **Status:** Verified by reading. The validator behaviour is pydantic's documented default and was
+  not executed.
+
+### WH-9. Storage and clean-up
+
+- **Default payload storage is local to the pod.** `PRIVATE_FILE_STORAGE` defaults to the local file
+  system. Without a private bucket configured, a payload file written by a web pod cannot be read
+  by a worker pod, and the send fails into WH-5. Verified, `saleor/settings.py:589-595`.
+- **Deliveries without a payload row are never cleaned up.** The daily clean-up deletes payloads and
+  relies on cascade. Deferred deliveries that failed before a payload was generated have none, so
+  the delivery table grows without bound. Verified for the clean-up task, `saleor/core/tasks.py:65-92`.
+- **Payload files are orphaned when the replica lags.** After a successful delivery the file list is
+  read from the replica while the rows are deleted on the primary. Files containing order and
+  customer data accumulate in the private bucket. Traced, `saleor/webhook/transport/utils.py:582-614`.
+- **Deleting a webhook deletes all its deliveries inside the request.** With a backlog this can time
+  out. Traced, `saleor/graphql/webhook/mutations/webhook_delete.py:82-86`.
+
+### WH-10. Synchronous webhooks can hold a request for a long time
+
+- `orderDiscountUpdate` calls the tax app inside an open database transaction, holding a connection
+  and a row lock for up to about 20 seconds when the app is slow. Traced,
+  `saleor/graphql/order/mutations/order_discount_update.py:78-92`.
+- The webhook timeout is per connect and per read, not total, and the whole response body is read
+  into memory. A target that trickles bytes can hold a request thread or worker slot. The circuit
+  breaker is off by default. Plausible, `saleor/webhook/transport/utils.py:246-287`.
+
+### WH-11. Behaviour to plan integrations around
+
+These are as designed, not defects.
+
+- **For some events, payloads show the state when they are built, not when the event happened**
+  (ADR 0011). The payload is generated later by a worker. Two quick changes produce two identical
+  deliveries; if the object is deleted first, the delivery fails and nothing is sent. An integration
+  cannot reconstruct per-refund amounts or intermediate states from these webhooks unless its
+  webhook is set to `IMMEDIATE`.
+
+  *Corrected on re-examination. The first version said this applied to "order, draft-order,
+  fulfillment and checkout events" as whole groups. It applies only to the 23 events flagged
+  `is_deferred_payload` in `saleor/webhook/event_types.py`:*
+
+  | Group | Built later by a worker | Built in the request, at the time of the event |
+  |---|---|---|
+  | Fulfillment | `FULFILLMENT_CANCELED`, `FULFILLMENT_METADATA_UPDATED`, `FULFILLMENT_TRACKING_NUMBER_UPDATED` | `FULFILLMENT_CREATED`, `FULFILLMENT_APPROVED` |
+  | Order | `ORDER_CREATED`, `ORDER_CONFIRMED`, `ORDER_PAID`, `ORDER_FULLY_PAID`, `ORDER_REFUNDED`, `ORDER_FULLY_REFUNDED`, `ORDER_UPDATED`, `ORDER_CANCELLED`, `ORDER_EXPIRED`, `ORDER_FULFILLED`, `ORDER_METADATA_UPDATED` | `ORDER_BULK_CREATED` |
+  | Draft order | `DRAFT_ORDER_CREATED`, `DRAFT_ORDER_UPDATED` | `DRAFT_ORDER_DELETED` |
+  | Checkout | `CHECKOUT_CREATED`, `CHECKOUT_UPDATED` | every other checkout event |
+  | Stock and price | the four per-channel in/out-of-stock events, `PRODUCT_VARIANT_DISCOUNTED_PRICE_UPDATED` | per-stock `PRODUCT_VARIANT_OUT_OF_STOCK` / `BACK_IN_STOCK` and everything else |
+
+  Even for the events in the middle column, the payload is built later only for webhooks with a
+  subscription query whose `payloadGeneration` is `DEFERRED` (the default). Webhooks without a
+  subscription query, and those set to `IMMEDIATE`, get a request-time payload. Verified,
+  `saleor/webhook/event_types.py`, `saleor/webhook/transport/asynchronous/transport.py:385`,
+  `420-436`.
+- **Retry policy:** five retries at 10, 20, 40, 80 and 160 seconds, then failed with no automatic
+  re-drive. 3xx and 4xx responses are never retried, including 429 and 408. Delivery is
+  at-least-once with no ordering guarantee.
+- **`issuedAt` on deferred events is the generation time**, not the request time.
+
